    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "title": "Soil Fertility",
      "text": "There are eight varieties of soil throughout the world. Their fertility differs, from very fertile to poor. Soil fertility is a very important property when it comes to farming.$(br)Soil fertility affects how strong fertilizer is: the more fertile the soil, the stronger its effect. Without fertilizer, crops grow at the same rate on every soil."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "With fertilizer, positive fertility increases the amount of nutrients applied, speeds up yield growth (never above its cap), and makes crops mature faster — maturing sooner also means maturing costs less fertilizer. Negative fertility does the opposite in every way.$(br)After fertilizing, those growth figures double or increase, with fertility stacking on top of them. Take Mollisol as an example: applying 10 units of nitrogen gives 12 units (20% fertility); after normal fertilizing, growth speed becomes 2x, and 2.2x on Mollisol (20% fertility); yield growth works the same way, but never exceeds the crop's own maximum harvest multiplier."
    },
